course: dictionary
lesson: 9955
title: Tattletale or telling?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A tattletale is a person, often a child, who tells a grown-up about other people's small mistakes, usually to get them into trouble. «gàozhuàngjīng» means tattletale, so the word is an informal, unkind name.
explain: Telling is different. Telling a grown-up when someone is hurt, in danger or being bullied keeps them safe. As the lesson says, telling a grown-up about real danger or bullying is right, whatever anyone calls you.
explain: Words change from place to place. In the USA and Canada, tattletale is common. In the UK and Australia, children say «telltale», «snitch», «tell-tale tit» or «dobber» for the same idea.
fact: A tattletale tells on people for small things, usually to get them into trouble.
fact: Telling a grown-up about real danger or bullying is telling to keep someone safe, not tattling.
fact: «Tattletale» is an informal, unkind name, mostly used in the USA and Canada.
fact: In the UK and Australia, children say «telltale», «snitch», «tell-tale tit» or «dobber».
fact: «gàozhuàngjīng» means tattletale in Chinese.
exercise: Ella tells Mum that Ben ate one biscuit before dinner. Is Ella being a tattletale? | *Yes, she is telling on a small mistake to get him in trouble | No, she is keeping him safe from real danger | No, she is saying hello | No, she is reporting a bully
exercise: «Some teasing is never fair: calling someone who reports real danger a tattletale.» Why is this unfair? | It means the person has a very long nose | *It can stop people telling an adult when they are really in danger | It is the same as saying well done | It shows the person is very funny
exercise: What might a child in Australia call a tattletale? | *Dobber | Whistle | Mate | Champion
test: Which is telling to keep someone safe, not tattling? | *Telling a grown-up that Jack is being bullied in the playground | Telling a joke about Jack's haircut | Telling the class that Jack wore odd socks | Telling Mum that Jack spilled juice on the rug
test: In the sentence «gàozhuàngjīng» means tattletale, what does the word mean? | A football champion | *Tattletale | A kind friend | A bubble
exam: Ruby sees a boy being pushed by the fence. What should she do, and why is it not tattling? | Keep quiet, because telling is always wrong | *Tell a grown-up, because he is hurt and telling keeps him safe | Tell the whole class about his lunch | Shout his name across the field
